<style>.last-updated-notice{background:#1a1a2e;border-left:4px solid #00d4ff;padding:12px 16px;margin-bottom:24px;font-size:14px;color:#ccc;border-radius:4px;}</style>
<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Tailscale free for personal use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Since Tailscale expanded the plan in April 2026, the Personal tier is free for up to 6 users with unlimited user-owned devices per user, according to Tailscale&#039;s own pricing page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Tailscale replace my existing firewall or VPN gateway?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It replaces the need for inbound port forwarding and a traditional site-to-site VPN gateway for the use cases in this tutorial, but it isn&#039;t a firewall replacement and doesn&#039;t inspect traffic content. Most teams run it alongside their existing security stack, including endpoint protection and a properly configured local firewall, rather than instead of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What&#039;s the real difference between Tailscale and a hand-configured WireGuard VPN?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tailscale runs on the WireGuard protocol underneath, but it automates key exchange, NAT traversal, and access rules that you&#039;d otherwise manage by hand in a plain WireGuard setup. The trade-off is that Tailscale&#039;s coordination happens through its hosted service by default, unless you self-host that piece with Headscale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I self-host the coordination server instead of using Tailscale&#039;s cloud?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, through Headscale, an open-source and community-maintained project that reimplements the coordination server while staying compatible with the standard Tailscale clients."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will Tailscale slow down my internet connection?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most connections are direct, device to device, with minimal overhead. Speeds only drop noticeably when a direct path isn&#039;t possible and traffic falls back to a relayed DERP connection, which the troubleshooting section above explains how to diagnose."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Tailscale secure enough for business use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It&#039;s built on WireGuard and layers identity-based ACLs, tagged access, optional tailnet lock, and Tailscale SSH on top, which reflects the same Zero Trust model described in NIST&#039;s SP 800-207. Whether that&#039;s sufficient for a given business depends on its own risk assessment and compliance obligations, but the underlying architecture matches current Zero Trust guidance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if Tailscale&#039;s coordination servers go down?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Devices that are already connected keep their existing peer-to-peer sessions. New connections, key exchanges, and ACL changes are what depend on reaching the control plane, which is also the main argument in favor of Headscale for teams that need to remove that dependency entirely."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I install Tailscale on a router or a NAS instead of a full computer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Beyond standard desktop, server, and mobile installs, Tailscale supports several NAS platforms and OpenWrt-based routers, which is a common way to turn an existing home router into the subnet router from Step 8 without adding another device."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is a Tailscale login different from a normal VPN username and password?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There isn&#039;t a separate Tailscale password to manage at all. Every login goes through the identity provider chosen in Step 1, so access to the tailnet rises and falls with that account. Disable someone&#039;s Google or Microsoft account and their access to every device on the tailnet disappears with it, without a separate offboarding step."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a paid plan once I go past six users?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Past six users, the Personal plan no longer applies and the tailnet needs to move to Standard or Premium, priced per user per month as shown in the pricing table above. The core setup from this tutorial, subnet routers, exit nodes, ACLs, and SSH, works the same way on every paid tier."}}]}</script>
    <style id="ti-toc-css">
    .ti-toc {
        background: #111827;
        border: 1px solid #1e3a5f;
        border-radius: 12px;
        padding: 20px 24px;
        margin: 28px 0;
        font-size: 0.92em;
        line-height: 1.6;
        transition: all 0.3s ease;
    }
    .ti-toc-header {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: center;
    }
    .ti-toc-title {
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
        font-size: 1.1em !important;
        font-weight: 700 !important;
        color: #e2e8f0 !important;
        background: none !important;
        border: none !important;
    }
    .ti-toc-toggle {
        background: none;
        border: 1px solid #374151;
        border-radius: 6px;
        color: #94a3b8;
        cursor: pointer;
        padding: 4px 8px;
        transition: all 0.2s;
    }
    .ti-toc-toggle:hover { border-color: #60a5fa; color: #60a5fa; }
    .ti-toc.collapsed .ti-toc-list { display: none; }
    .ti-toc.collapsed .ti-toc-toggle svg { transform: rotate(-90deg); }

    .ti-toc-list {
        margin: 14px 0 0 !important;
        padding-left: 0 !important;
        list-style: none !important;
        counter-reset: toc;
    }
    .ti-toc-list > li {
        counter-increment: toc;
        padding: 6px 0 6px 0;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #1e293b;
        position: relative;
    }
    .ti-toc-list > li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
    .ti-toc-list > li::before {
        content: counter(toc) "." !important;
        color: #7c3aed !important;
        font-weight: 700;
        margin-right: 8px;
        position: static !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
    }
    .ti-toc-list a {
        color: #94a3b8 !important;
        text-decoration: none !important;
        transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    .ti-toc-list a:hover {
        color: #60a5fa !important;
    }

    /* H3 sub-items */
    .ti-toc-sub {
        margin: 4px 0 4px 24px !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
        list-style: none !important;
        counter-reset: tocsub;
    }
    .ti-toc-sub li {
        padding: 3px 0;
    }
    .ti-toc-sub li::before {
        content: "–" !important;
        color: #4b5563 !important;
        margin-right: 6px;
        position: static !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
    }
    .ti-toc-sub a {
        color: #6b7280 !important;
        font-size: 0.93em;
    }
    .ti-toc-sub a:hover { color: #60a5fa !important; }

    /* Smooth scroll */
    html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
    h2[id], h3[id] { scroll-margin-top: 80px; }
    </style>
    <noscript><link rel='stylesheet' href='https://tech-insider.org/ca/wp-content/themes/blocksy/static/bundle/no-scripts.min.css' type='text/css'></noscript>
